## A spotlight on the past

A model uses <GlossaryPopover term="attention">attention</GlossaryPopover> to let each token look
back over everything before it and pull in what's useful. Picture each token holding a spotlight. It
shines on the earlier tokens, brightly on the relevant ones and dimly on the rest, and takes in a
blend of whatever it lights up. Attention is the core idea of the <GlossaryPopover term="transformer">transformer</GlossaryPopover>, the design introduced in 2017
that almost every modern LLM uses, GPT-2 included.<Cite id="vaswani2017" />

## Queries, keys, and values

How does a token decide where to shine? Each token's vector is turned into three new vectors, using
weights learned in training:

- a **query**: what this token is looking for,
- a **key**: what this token has to offer, to anyone looking,
- a **value**: what this token passes on if it gets picked.

To score how relevant one token is to another, multiply the first token's query with the second
token's key, number by number, and add up the results. When the two vectors point in similar
directions (think of the nearby words in <ChapterRef to="embeddings" />), the score is high. The scores are divided by a fixed number
to keep them from getting too large, then softmax (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />) turns them into percentages. Finally each
token takes a weighted blend of the values.

Step through it with made-up numbers:

<QKVExplainer />

Notice the blank cells: a token can't look at tokens that come after it. When the model is
predicting the next word, those words don't exist yet.

## Real attention in GPT-2

Here are attention patterns recorded from GPT-2. Each line runs from the word doing the looking
(right) to an earlier word (left); thicker lines mean more attention.

<AttentionViewer />

Some patterns are surprisingly readable:

- **Who gets the drink?** In "When Mary and John went to the store, John gave a drink to", GPT-2
  predicts " Mary" next. Layer 9, head 9 looks from the last word back at "Mary" far more than at
  either "John" (the pull toward "When" is explained below).
  Researchers who took this behavior apart found a small set of heads, including 9.6 and 9.9, that
  find the right name and copy it forward.<Cite id="wang2022" />
- **Whose fleece?** In the nursery rhyme, layer 9, head 0 looks from "its" back to "lamb".
- **What was too big?** The same head looks from "it" mostly to "suitcase", in *both* versions of
  the trophy sentence. That isn't a mistake in the viewer: "big" and "small" come after "it", and a
  word can only look back. The model has to sort out "it" later, at the words after the clue.

GPT-2 isn't very good at that last kind of puzzle. Even the largest GPT-2 answered 70.70% of
Winograd questions correctly, where guessing gets 50%.<Cite id="radford2019" /> The patterns you can
read are a few bright spots in a mostly murky picture.

## Many heads, many layers

One set of queries, keys, and values is called an <GlossaryPopover term="attention-head">attention head</GlossaryPopover>. Each layer of GPT-2 runs
12 heads side by side, each with its own weights, so each can look for something different. With
12 layers, that's 144 heads. Layers and heads are numbered from 0, as in research papers.

Switch to other heads in the viewer and you'll see that many don't have a job you can name. You'll
also see lots of heads putting most of their attention on the very first word. In these sentences,
from the fourth word on, that happens more than half the time. Researchers think it's a resting
spot: softmax always has to share out 100%, so a head with nothing useful to find parks its
attention there.<Cite id="xiao2024" />

<RecapCard>

- Attention lets each token pull in information from any earlier token, weighted by relevance.
- Each token makes a query, a key, and a value. Query times key gives a score; softmax turns scores
  into weights; the weights blend the values.
- A token can only look back, never ahead.
- GPT-2 has 12 heads in each of its 12 layers. A few have clear jobs, like finding the name that
  comes next; most are harder to read.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** attention moves information between tokens. How does it fit into a whole model?
